# Firmware Card Purity

MCU card code (`channel_card` / `effect_card` application sources) is a
**standalone product surface**. Comments, headers, and public names describe
what the card does — sizes, rates, protocol verbs, hazards — not how a Mac
app or generator script happens to produce payloads.

## Do

- Document card facts in card units: `BODY_BANK_LEN` samples @ 48 kHz, CDC
  `bl`/`al`/`ar`, `phase_inc = note_hz / root_hz`.
- Name USB roles abstractly when needed (`UAC IN`, `CDC host`, `upstream
  MIDI session`) — not repo paths or binary names.
- Keep host/tools as the side that must match the card: generators and
  uploaders cite firmware constants / protocol, never the reverse.

## Do not

- Reference host tree paths or tools in card comments
  (`tools/gen_sample_waves.py`, `cmi_control/...`, `sample48/`).
- Name host binaries or libs as authorities (`cmi_control`,
  `cardlink`) in card comments — describe the failure/protocol instead.
- Encode “matches the GUI/script” as the reason for a `#define`; state the
  hardware/audio constraint (length, sample rate, loop period).

```c
/* BAD — card documents the host tree */
/** Loop length — matches tools/gen_sample_waves.py. */
#define BODY_BANK_LEN 9600u

/* GOOD — card owns the contract */
/** Samples per sustain loop @ 48 kHz (~200 ms). */
#define BODY_BANK_LEN 9600u
```

Host-side docs may say “must match `BODY_BANK_LEN` on the card.” Card-side
docs must not say “must match the Python generator.”
